var _markdown = "\n\n`@kwiva/react` binds the router to React and owns the framework's SSR orchestration. It exports `definePage` (the complete page contract), the streaming renderer and its hydration contract, the `Providers` tree, and the data hooks that give components typed, cache-backed access to models. React is the default runtime; Preact is a config flip.\n\n## definePage [#definepage]\n\nEvery page file under `src/ui/pages/` default-exports a `definePage` definition. The factory takes a single options object covering validation, data, guards, and UI states.\n\n```tsx title=\"definepage.tsx\"\nimport { definePage, redirect, stream } from '@kwiva/react'\n\nexport default definePage({\n  validateSearch: (s) => s.object({ tab: s.optional(s.string()) }),\n\n  loader: async ({ params, client }) => ({\n    post: await client.posts.get(params.id),\n    comments: stream(client.comments.list({ postId: params.id })),\n  }),\n\n  loaderDeps: ({ search: { tab } }) => ({ tab }),\n\n  beforeLoad: ({ session, location }) => {\n    if (!session.user) throw redirect({ to: '/login', search: { back: location.href } })\n  },\n\n  pendingComponent: PostSkeleton,\n  pendingMs: 200,\n  pendingMinMs: 300,\n\n  errorComponent: ({ error }) => <ErrorPanel error={error} />,\n  notFoundComponent: () => <p>No such post</p>,\n\n  head: ({ loaderData }) => ({\n    title: loaderData.post.title,\n    meta: [{ name: 'description', content: loaderData.post.excerpt }],\n  }),\n\n  component: PostPage,\n})\n```\n\n### Options [#options]\n\n| Option              | Type                              | Description                                                                              |\n| ------------------- | --------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `component`         | `(props) => ReactNode`            | Renders the page; receives `loaderData`, `params`, and `search`                          |\n| `loader`            | `(ctx) => Promise<LoaderData>`    | Server- and client-side data fetch for the route; runs in parallel across matched routes |\n| `loaderDeps`        | `(ctx) => Deps`                   | Selects which navigational fields refetch the loader when they change                    |\n| `beforeLoad`        | `(ctx) => void \\| Promise<void>`  | Guard running before loaders; throws `redirect` or `notFound` to abort                   |\n| `validateSearch`    | `(s) => Schema`                   | Search-param schema via a Standard Schema validator builder                              |\n| `pendingComponent`  | `Component`                       | Rendered during load on client-side navigation                                           |\n| `pendingMs`         | `number`                          | Delay before the pending UI shows                                                        |\n| `pendingMinMs`      | `number`                          | Minimum time the pending UI stays visible                                                |\n| `errorComponent`    | `({ error }) => ReactNode`        | Rendered when the loader or guards throw                                                 |\n| `notFoundComponent` | `() => ReactNode`                 | Rendered when `notFound()` is thrown or no route matches                                 |\n| `head`              | `({ loaderData }) => HeadOptions` | Per-route document head, serialized during SSR                                           |\n\n### loader [#loader]\n\nThe loader is the only data-fetching surface for a page. It executes server-side during SSR (in-process, via the typed client) and client-side on navigation, in parallel across all matched routes and deduplicated per request.\n\n```tsx title=\"loader.tsx\"\nloader: async ({ params, search, client }) => ({\n  post: await client.posts.get(params.id),\n  comments: stream(client.comments.list({ postId: params.id, page: search.page })),\n})\n```\n\n| Loader context | Description                                |\n| -------------- | ------------------------------------------ |\n| `params`       | Typed route `$param` values                |\n| `search`       | Typed, validated search params             |\n| `client`       | Typed RPC client injected by the framework |\n\nA promise wrapped in `stream()` is deferred: the awaited shell renders immediately while the deferred value streams into its `Suspense` boundary. Loader results become `loaderData` and are written to the data-hook cache, so render and later hooks never double-fetch. See [Loaders & Data](/docs/frontend/loaders) and [Streaming SSR](/docs/rendering/streaming).\n\n### loaderDeps [#loaderdeps]\n\n`loaderDeps` returns the subset of route state that should trigger a refetch. Navigation that changes only non-dep fields reuses the cached loader result.\n\n```tsx title=\"loaderdeps.tsx\"\nloaderDeps: ({ search: { page } }) => ({ page })\n```\n\n### beforeLoad [#beforeload]\n\nGuards run before loaders, on both SSR and the client. The context carries `session` and `location`; throwing `redirect` or `notFound` short-circuits the request.\n\n```tsx title=\"beforeload.tsx\"\nbeforeLoad: ({ session }) => {\n  if (!session.user) throw redirect({ to: '/login' })\n}\n```\n\n### validateSearch [#validatesearch]\n\nThe search schema is built with a Standard Schema validator builder. It types `search`, `Link` and `useNavigate` targets, and drives `loaderDeps` refetching — invalid values fail at compile time.\n\n```tsx title=\"validatesearch.tsx\"\nvalidateSearch: (s) => s.object({ q: s.optional(s.string()), page: s.optional(s.number()) })\n```\n\n### head [#head]\n\n`head` returns the document head for the route and is serialized per route during SSR, so SEO tags can derive from loader data.\n\n```tsx title=\"head.tsx\"\nhead: ({ loaderData }) => ({\n  title: loaderData.post.title,\n  meta: [{ name: 'description', content: loaderData.post.excerpt }],\n})\n```\n\n## SSR Renderer & Streaming [#ssr-renderer--streaming]\n\n`@kwiva/react` owns SSR orchestration. The framework renders; the serving engine responds. The pipeline is:\n\n```plaintext title=\"ssr-renderer-streaming.txt\"\nrequest (page route)\n  → router match (owned router)\n  → beforeLoad guards (session → login redirects)\n  → loaders execute in parallel (server-side, via the typed client, in-process)\n  → React tree renders to a readable stream\n      ├─ Suspense boundaries stream as they resolve (stream() deferreds)\n      └─ loader state dehydrates into the page at the stream tail\n  → response stream (http status, headers, set-cookies)\nhydration (client)\n  → state rehydrates into the data-hook cache (no refetch of loader data)\n  → router resumes at the same route with the same search state\n```\n\n* The blocking shell renders immediately; deferred chunks hydrate into their `Suspense` boundaries as they resolve.\n* Route rules apply to the whole response: `isr` caches the full stream, `swr` serves stale while revalidating, `static` builds once.\n* Loader, shell, and hydration phases are exposed as OTel spans (`SSR wall time`, time-to-first-byte, shell time, hydration time). See [Tracing](/docs/observability/tracing).\n\n### Route rules [#route-rules]\n\nPage routes honor the same rules as the API surface, declared per path in `src/routes/rules.ts`:\n\n| Rule              | Behavior                                              |\n| ----------------- | ----------------------------------------------------- |\n| (default)         | Dynamic SSR each request                              |\n| `cache: n`        | Full response cached for `n` seconds                  |\n| `swr: n`          | Stale-while-revalidate (revalidate in the background) |\n| `isr: n`          | Static page regenerated on an interval or on demand   |\n| `static`          | Built once at `kwiva build`                           |\n| `prerender: true` | Crawled/prerendered at build time                     |\n\n```ts title=\"route-rules.ts\"\ndefineServerRoute('/products/**', { isr: 300, cache: { tags: ['catalog'] } })\n```\n\nOn-demand invalidation (`invalidateTags(['catalog'])`) is available from any job, task, or event handler. See [Caching Strategies](/docs/rendering/caching).\n\n### Personalization with cached shells [#personalization-with-cached-shells]\n\nPublic shells can be cached (`isr`) while user-specific fragments load client-side through data hooks — session-scoped reads are never part of the cached shell.\n\n### SSR error handling [#ssr-error-handling]\n\n* Loader or guard throws render that route's `errorComponent`, or the root boundary.\n* Deferred promise rejections surface in their boundary with a fallback and retry.\n* In dev, the overlay attaches the loader stack and the OTel trace id.\n\n### SPA fallback [#spa-fallback]\n\nIn `api+spa` mode, the same `definePage` files render client-only: the server serves the shell plus the API, and route rules still apply to the API surface.\n\n## Hydration Contract [#hydration-contract]\n\nHydration is island-free and streaming-safe:\n\n* Loader data and deferred promises serialize with a typed reviver — dates and class instances such as model rows survive the wire.\n* The client data-hook cache is keyed identically on server and client, so hydration is a **cache transfer**, not a refetch.\n* Dehydrated state is written into the page as `<script id=\"kwiva:state\">` at the stream tail; the client rehydrates it into the cache before first render.\n* The router resumes at the same route with the same search state.\n\nSee [Hydration](/docs/rendering/hydration) and [SSR](/docs/rendering/ssr).\n\n## Providers [#providers]\n\n`Providers` mounts the framework context at the root layout (`src/ui/pages/__root.tsx`). It injects session, the typed client, the data-hook cache, i18n, and theme, and handles client-side session and CSRF wiring.\n\n```tsx title=\"providers.tsx\"\nimport { definePage, Providers, Outlet } from '@kwiva/react'\n\nexport default definePage({\n  component: () => (\n    <Providers theme=\"kwiva\" i18n={catalog}>\n      <Layout>\n        <Outlet />\n      </Layout>\n    </Providers>\n  ),\n})\n```\n\n| Provider prop | Description                                        |\n| ------------- | -------------------------------------------------- |\n| `theme`       | Active theme key; overrides the configured default |\n| `i18n`        | Typed translation catalog (v1.x)                   |\n\n## Data Hooks [#data-hooks]\n\nData hooks give components typed access to models over a client-side cache. Their keys derive from **model identity plus parameters**, so invalidation is model-scoped instead of hand-built.\n\n```tsx title=\"data-hooks.tsx\"\nimport { useResource, useList, useMutation, useInfiniteList, invalidate, useApi } from '@kwiva/react'\nimport { Post } from '@kwiva/data'\n```\n\n| Hook                               | Purpose                                      | Key                     |\n| ---------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------- | ----------------------- |\n| `useResource(Model, id, options?)` | Read a single record                         | Model identity + id     |\n| `useList(Model, options)`          | Paginated, filtered list                     | Model identity + params |\n| `useMutation(action, options?)`    | Create/update/delete with optimistic updates | —                       |\n| `useInfiniteList(Model, options)`  | Cursor-based infinite list                   | Model identity + cursor |\n| `useApi(key, fetcher)`             | Custom query over a typed fetcher            | Explicit key            |\n| `invalidate(Model)`                | Clear every derived key for a model          | —                       |\n\n### useResource [#useresource]\n\n```tsx title=\"useresource.tsx\"\nconst { data, isPending, error } = useResource(Post, id)\nconst { data: post } = useResource(Post, id, { suspense: true })\n```\n\n### useList [#uselist]\n\n```tsx title=\"uselist.tsx\"\nconst { data, isFetching } = useList(Post, {\n  where: { status: 'published' },\n  orderBy: { createdAt: 'desc' },\n  staleTime: 30_000,\n})\n```\n\n### useMutation [#usemutation]\n\nMutations wrap a model action, support optimistic updates, and auto-invalidate on success.\n\n```tsx title=\"usemutation.tsx\"\nconst save = useMutation(Post.update, {\n  optimistic: (input, current) => ({ ...current, ...input }),\n  onSuccess: () => invalidate(Post),\n})\n\nconst { mutate, isMutating } = save\nawait mutate({ title: 'New title' })\n```\n\nThe `optimistic` helper receives the mutation input and the currently cached row, and returns the next value; on failure the cache rolls back. Successful mutations invalidate model-scoped keys unless told otherwise.\n\n### useInfiniteList [#useinfinitelist]\n\n```tsx title=\"useinfinitelist.tsx\"\nconst feed = useInfiniteList(Post, { cursor: 'createdAt', limit: 20 })\n```\n\n### useApi [#useapi]\n\nThe escape hatch for custom queries backed by the typed client:\n\n```tsx title=\"useapi.tsx\"\nconst report = useApi(['report', id], () => client.reports.get(id))\n```\n\n### Cache options [#cache-options]\n\nThe full data-hook option surface is passed through on read hooks:\n\n| Option                 | Meaning                                                 |\n| ---------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `staleTime`            | How long a result stays fresh before background refetch |\n| `gcTime`               | How long an unused entry stays in the cache             |\n| `refetchOnWindowFocus` | Refetch when the window regains focus                   |\n| `refetchInterval`      | Polling interval                                        |\n| `suspense`             | Suspend while pending                                   |\n| `retry`                | Retry policy on failure                                 |\n| `select`               | Derive a view of the data                               |\n| `placeholderData`      | Show data while refetching                              |\n| `enabled`              | Gate the query                                          |\n\n### Invalidation model [#invalidation-model]\n\n* `invalidate(Model)` clears every list, detail, and custom-key entry derived from that model.\n* `invalidateTags([...])` (server-side) clears route-rule response caches. See [Caching](/docs/rendering/caching).\n* Loader results, navigation preloads, and hook reads share one cache, so no path double-fetches. See [Hydration](/docs/rendering/hydration) and [Routing](/api/router).\n\n### Devtools [#devtools]\n\n`@kwiva/react/devtools` mounts a framework-skinned devtools pane for inspecting cache entries, keys, and staleness. The dev overlay shows loader timings and cache state during development.\n\n## Client Hooks [#client-hooks]\n\nThe provider exposes typed client hooks:\n\n```tsx title=\"client-hooks.tsx\"\nconst { user, signOut, isPending } = useSession()\nconst theme = useConfig('ui.theme')\nconst canPublish = useCan('posts.publish')\nconst t = useT()\n```\n\n| Hook                 | Description                                                  |\n| -------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `useSession()`       | Auth state, typed user, and sign-in/out helpers              |\n| `useConfig(key)`     | Typed reads of client config; `KWIVA_PUBLIC_*` keys only     |\n| `useCan(permission)` | Client-mirrored policy check, e.g. `useCan('posts.publish')` |\n| `useT()`             | Typed i18n catalog access (v1.x)                             |\n\nSee [Client-Side Auth](/docs/auth/client-usage) and [Policies](/docs/authorization/policies).\n\n## Realtime Hooks [#realtime-hooks]\n\nRealtime hooks wrap typed channel subscriptions from the client:\n\n```tsx title=\"realtime-hooks.tsx\"\nconst { messages, send } = useChannel(`chat.${roomId}`, {\n  onMessage: (m) => enqueue(m),\n})\n\nconst { members } = usePresence(`chat.${roomId}`)\n```\n\n| Hook                           | Description                                                        |\n| ------------------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `useChannel(channel, options)` | Subscribe to a channel; returns the event list and a `send` helper |\n| `usePresence(channel)`         | Track channel presence; returns the member set                     |\n\nSee [Client-Side Realtime](/docs/realtime/client-usage) and [Channels](/docs/realtime/channels).\n\n## Forms [#forms]\n\n`useForm` builds schema-driven forms from a model action. Fields derive from model validation, server errors map back to fields, and submit runs through the mutation pipeline on the same cache — no separate form library is required (v1.x).\n\n```tsx title=\"forms.tsx\"\nconst form = useForm(Post.create)\n```\n\n## Preact Compatibility [#preact-compatibility]\n\n`@kwiva/react` supports a Preact runtime through `preact/compat`, selected in the UI config (`src/config/ui.ts`):\n\n```ts title=\"preact-compatibility.ts\"\nimport { defineConfig } from '@kwiva/config'\n\nexport default defineConfig('ui', {\n  defaults: { runtime: 'react', theme: 'kwiva' },\n})\n```\n\nSetting `runtime: 'preact'` makes the framework's Rust-speed toolchain rewrite React import specifiers to `preact/compat` at build time — one config flip, application code unchanged. The pipeline, providers, data hooks, and hydration contract behave identically with a smaller bundle.\n\n## What to Read Next [#what-to-read-next]\n\n* [Pages](/docs/frontend/pages) — `definePage` walkthrough\n* [Data Hooks](/docs/frontend/data-hooks) — Hook reference by use case\n* [SSR](/docs/rendering/ssr) — Render pipeline and route rules\n* [Streaming SSR](/docs/rendering/streaming) — Deferred data in depth\n* [Hydration](/docs/rendering/hydration) — The cache-transfer contract\n* [Nested Layouts](/docs/frontend/layouts) — Root layout with `Providers`\n* [Client-Side Realtime](/docs/realtime/client-usage) — `useChannel` and `usePresence`\n* [Client-Side Auth](/docs/auth/client-usage) — `useSession`\n* [`@kwiva/router`](/api/router) — Routing conventions, loaders, and navigation\n* [`@kwiva/client`](/api/client) — The typed RPC client under the hooks\n";
